The first half of this fix recovered an external receiver's module path
through use_aliases, which works only when the type was imported BY NAME.
When the source spells the path instead -- `fn dump(f: &mut std::fs::File)`,
or `let start = std::time::Instant::now()` with no `use` --
_normalize_rust_type_to_bare_name reduces it to `File` / `Instant` ("strip
module paths", per its own docstring, which is right for the first-party
symbol lookups it feeds) and the bare name is then absent from use_aliases.
The path was in the text and was dropped anyway.

A second file-scoped map carries the written path beside the bare one and is
consulted only on the unresolved-external branch, where a bare name is
useless. It cannot invent a path: a value is returned only when the text
already contains `::` AND the path's terminal segment equals the bare name
the normalizer produced. That equality binds the two readings of one type --
`std::io::Result<std::fs::File>` normalizes to `File` while the text starts
with `Result`, so it yields nothing rather than stamping the wrong module and
converting a correct L4 refusal into a confident mismatch.

MEASURED A/B over 11 Rust repositories, per-arm cache, arms strictly
sequential, each arm proven live before it ran:

  catalogued trait-method sites matched   41/1202 -> 98/1206  (3.4% -> 8.1%)
  rust call sites given a boundary        303 -> 360          (+18.8%)
    host_info_read                        68 -> 116
    fs_read                               50 -> 59
  CONTROLS, unchanged: env_read 29, env_write 19, fs_write 63,
    ipc_recv 7, logging 15, subprocess 52

IT MOVES NO TAINT FLOW -- 2 -> 2 -- and the reason is structural, not
incidental. 48 of the 57 new matches are Instant::elapsed, a host_info_read,
which IS an auto-derived source but reaches no write-side sink here; the
other 9 are fs_read rows, which AUTO_SOURCE_LABEL_MAP excludes by design
because reading a file does not by itself make its contents sensitive. This
is an io-boundaries improvement and is reported as one.

STILL BLOCKED, named rather than left implicit: write_all did not move (5
matched, 125 missing) and `lock` is the largest miss at 440. Both are
dominated by two receiver shapes this does not reach, because they lose the
type further upstream in the receiver walk rather than in normalization -- a
struct-field receiver and a chained receiver. Filed separately; each needs
its own measurement.

